From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 17:55:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hostiface: accept the BIOS attribute registry POST

A full request log of the TP26 BIOS's host-interface push (bmcweb built with
-Dbmcweb-logging=debug, captured 2026-08-11) shows a POST we answer with 404:

    POST /redfish/v1/Registries/BiosAttributeRegistryTPCH.0.52.0.json  404

The OEM BMC answers this with 201.  The registry is worth keeping on its own
merits: it describes every BIOS setup attribute, which is what a Redfish client
needs in order to render /redfish/v1/Systems/<id>/Bios.

The registry POST is handled by hooking requestRoutesMessageRegistryFile()
rather than by adding a route.  bmcweb keys its routing trie by (path, method);
/redfish/v1/Registries/<str>/ is ALREADY registered for POST/PATCH/DELETE by
that function, and adding a second registration for the same (path, method)
throws at startup -- bmcweb then core-dumps in a restart loop until systemd
gives up, taking Redfish down completely.  Confirmed the hard way on hardware:

    [CRITICAL routing.hpp:293] handler already exists for
        "/redfish/v1/Registries/<str>/"
    terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::runtime_error'

Note that the duplicate-registration check is compiled out when any of the AMI
feature macros (BMCWEB_AMI_REP_MACRO and friends) are set, so this trap is
invisible in some configurations and fatal in ours.

The hook is scoped to registry names beginning "BiosAttributeRegistry", so the
405/404 behaviour of every other registry is unchanged; previously such a POST
fell through to resourceNotFound.

DynamicExtension: DO NOT ANSWER THIS.
-------------------------------------
An earlier revision of this patch also added a route for

    POST /redfish/v1/DynamicExtension/RedfishExtensions

which the BIOS sends four times before anything else, and answered it 201 so
the payloads could be captured.  That change made the host UNBOOTABLE.

With the route in place the BIOS accepted the 201s, continued through the
registry and static-file pushes, and then died in its own Redfish inventory
driver at the firmware-layout push:

    !!!! X64 Exception Type - 0D (#GP - General Protection) !!!!
    ...\p26c\14203m\...\RfInventory\DEBUG\RfInventory.pdb

POST code 98, BDS reached, never hands off to a boot device.  Reproduced on two
different BIOS chips (both TPC_P26C), which is what proves it is the BMC side:
the BIOS never changed.  With the earlier 404 behaviour (captured 2026-08-11)
the BIOS tolerated the rejection and carried on -- it stopped early without
pushing inventory, but it BOOTED.

The OEM BMC does not serve this endpoint at all; it is absent from the OEM
lighttpd access log entirely, so on the OEM the BIOS gets a 404 here and moves
on.  Answering affirmatively appears to switch the BIOS onto an extension path
it then faults on.  Matching the OEM means NOT answering.

The four payloads are already captured under /var/lib/flax-inventory/dynext/
from the run that crashed, so nothing is lost by reverting to 404.  If they are
ever needed again, capture them with a passive proxy rather than by answering
201 from bmcweb.

Signed-off-by: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
---
--- a/redfish-core/lib/flax_host_interface.hpp
+++ b/redfish-core/lib/flax_host_interface.hpp
@@ -214,6 +214,24 @@
                      boost::beast::http::status::no_content);
 }
 
+// The TP26 BIOS POSTs its BIOS attribute registry to
+//     POST /redfish/v1/Registries/BiosAttributeRegistryTPCH.0.52.0.json
+// and the OEM answers 201; we answered 404 (captured 2026-08-11).  Keep the
+// file -- it describes every BIOS setup attribute, which is what a Redfish
+// client needs to render /redfish/v1/Systems/<id>/Bios.
+//
+// This is called from message_registries.hpp rather than from a route of our
+// own: bmcweb keys its routing trie by (path, method), that path is already
+// registered for POST by requestRoutesMessageRegistryFile(), and registering
+// it twice aborts bmcweb at startup with "handler already exists".
+inline void handleFlaxRegistryUpload(
+    crow::App& app, const crow::Request& req,
+    const std::shared_ptr<bmcweb::AsyncResp>& asyncResp)
+{
+    handleFlaxUpload(app, req, asyncResp, "registry", "bios-registry.json",
+                     false, boost::beast::http::status::created);
+}
+
 inline void handleFlaxBiosBlobUpload(
     crow::App& app, const crow::Request& req,
     const std::shared_ptr<bmcweb::AsyncResp>& asyncResp)
--- a/redfish-core/lib/message_registries.hpp
+++ b/redfish-core/lib/message_registries.hpp
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 #pragma once
 
 #include "app.hpp"
+#include "flax_host_interface.hpp"
 #include "query.hpp"
 #include "registries.hpp"
 #include "registries/base_message_registry.hpp"
@@ -560,6 +561,18 @@
     {
         return;
     }
+    // The TP26 BIOS POSTs its BIOS attribute registry here, e.g.
+    //   POST /redfish/v1/Registries/BiosAttributeRegistryTPCH.0.52.0.json
+    // That name is not one of the built-in registries listed below, so it fell
+    // through to resourceNotFound and 404'd.  The OEM BMC answers 201.  Store
+    // it instead.  Scoped to the BiosAttributeRegistry prefix so the 405/404
+    // behaviour of every other registry is unchanged.
+    if (req.method() == boost::beast::http::verb::post &&
+        registry.starts_with("BiosAttributeRegistry"))
+    {
+        handleFlaxRegistryUpload(app, req, asyncResp);
+        return;
+    }
     if (!membersResponseGet(asyncResp, registry, "MessageRegistryFileCollection"))
     {
         return;
